; FIXED-LIMB BIGNUM ARITHMETIC, WRITTEN IN OROBOROS.
;
; This is the rung ADR 0019's endpoint spelling exists to name and which nothing
; yet selects: a FINITE range above the portable window gives a limb count,
; which gives a `build` of known length, which gives ZERO ALLOCATIONS.
; bigarith-2026-08-28 measured that form at 3.97x over `math/big` on Go, 6.2x
; over `BigInteger` and 5.8x over `BigInt` -- and on Java and V8 it is the ONLY
; way to get there, because those bignums are immutable.
;
; It is written here as an ordinary program first, because the question that
; decides everything downstream is whether the language can express it at all.
; general-purpose.md claims it can; `emit/winmap.oro` is the one existing piece
; of evidence, and a hash table is a much easier shape than a carry chain.
;
; ═══ BASE 2^24, AND THE BASE IS FORCED RATHER THAN CHOSEN
;
; A limb product plus the accumulated carry has to stay EXACT, and ADR 0012 says
; an `int` is exact to +-(2^53-1). With W-bit limbs a product is under 2^2W, so
; 2W < 53 and W <= 26. Taking W = 24 leaves room to spare:
;
;   out[i+j] < 2^24, a[i]*b[j] < 2^48, carry < 2^25
;   t = out + a*b + carry  <  2^48 + 2^25 + 2^24  <  2^49
;
; so the carry stays under 2^25 forever and nothing approaches 2^53.
;
; ═══ `/` AND `%`, NOT A SHIFT AND A MASK, AND THAT IS THE LANGUAGE SPEAKING
;
; The natural way to split a limb is `t >> 24` and `t & 0xFFFFFF`. Bitwise
; operators are DELIBERATELY not in the language (integers.md §0a): V8 coerces
; both operands to int32, so `(2^32) & -1` is 0 there and 4294967296 on the
; other three -- an observable disagreement INSIDE the portable window, which is
; what makes a construct Tier 2.
;
; So this uses `/` and `%`, which every host agrees on. That is a real cost and
; bigarith measured it: bitwise carry extraction is worth 3.9x at equal storage
; on V8. It is the price of writing the library once instead of four times, and
; naming it here is what makes it a decision rather than an oversight.
;
; ═══ WHAT IS AND IS NOT HERE
;
; Non-negative magnitudes only. `(int 0 +inf)` and `(int 0 (pow 2 N))` are the
; declarations this serves and both are non-negative, and bigarith measured sign
; handling as a real cost -- so the half-open range carries something the
; representation spends, which is unbounded-rung.md §4.2's argument for `+inf`
; over a bare type name.
;
; Decimal output is not here. It needs repeated division by a power of ten and
; then a string, and strings are the one thing this language has almost none of.

; NO `(use …)` AT ALL: every operator here is the language's, so this file names
; no host and generates on all four targets unchanged. That is what integers.md
; §0a bought — before the promotion this would have had to be written four
; times, once per host's spelling of `+`.
(export limb)

; ---------------------------------------------------------------- the base
;
; Written out rather than computed, because it appears in every carry split and
; reduction folds a literal where it would have to keep a computation.
;   2^24 = 16777216

; A CLAMP, exactly as tree.oro, winmap.oro and kara/core.oro use one. Every
; index here is derived from a loop bound over a DIFFERENT table than the one
; being read -- `a` and `b` are separate buffers under one bound -- which is the
; latent bug the refinement layer found in `dot` and `centroid`. Two tables have
; the same length by construction and nothing can see that, so the program says
; what happens if they do not.
(def at (fn (t i) (if (< i 0) 0 (if (>= i (len t)) 0 (t i)))))

; ---------------------------------------------------------------- of a word
;
; A machine word spread over `n` limbs, least significant first.
(def bn-of (fn (n v)
  (build n (fn (b)
    (loop ((b b) (i 0) (r v))
      (>= i n)  b
      else      (again (set b i (% r 16777216)) (+ i 1) (/ r 16777216)))))))

; ---------------------------------------------------------------- addition
;
; a + b, into a buffer of the caller's length. A sum needs at most one limb more
; than its longer operand, and `build` takes that length up front -- which is
; the whole reason a bignum needs no growable storage: every result's size is
; computable from its operands' (bigarith §"a bignum needs NO growable storage").
;
; The carry rides as an ordinary loop variable, which is what makes this
; expressible at all: `again` may sit under a `let`, so the sum can be named
; once and used for both the stored limb and the outgoing carry.
(def bn-add (fn (n a b)
  (build n (fn (o)
    (loop ((o o) (i 0) (c 0))
      (>= i n)  o
      else      (let (+ (+ (at a i) (at b i)) c) (fn (t)
                  (again (set o i (% t 16777216)) (+ i 1) (/ t 16777216)))))))))

; ------------------------------------------------------- multiply by a word
;
; a * k for k < 2^24, which is the operation a factorial is made of and the one
; bigarith measured our form winning by 3.97x. One pass, one carry.
;
; `(at a i) * k` is under 2^48 and the carry under 2^25, so the sum is under
; 2^49 -- inside the window with four bits to spare.
(def bn-mul-small (fn (n a k)
  (build n (fn (o)
    (loop ((o o) (i 0) (c 0))
      (>= i n)  o
      else      (let (+ (* (at a i) k) c) (fn (t)
                  (again (set o i (% t 16777216)) (+ i 1) (/ t 16777216)))))))))

; ---------------------------------------------------------------- factorial
;
; n! in `w` limbs. Two buffers are live -- the accumulator and the one
; `bn-mul-small` builds -- and the accumulator is replaced each step, which is
; ADR 0018's linearity: `build` freezes on the way out, so what the loop carries
; is an ordinary immutable `array` and the next iteration reads it.
;
; It allocates one buffer per iteration, which is what the reuse question is
; about and is deliberately left alone here: the question this file answers is
; whether the language can EXPRESS the arithmetic.
(def bn-fact (fn (w n)
  (loop ((acc (bn-of w 1)) (i 2))
    (> i n)  acc
    else     (again (bn-mul-small w acc i) (+ i 1)))))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------ harness
;
; `limb k` is limb k of 200!, checked against `math/big` and against `BigInt`
; and `BigInteger` -- ORACLES rather than another implementation of ours -- by
; `gauntlet/differential/cases/big-limbs.oro`. 200! is about 1,245 bits, so 52
; limbs are significant and 64 is a comfortable buffer.
(def limb (fn (k) (at (bn-fact 64 200) k)))
